1
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:06,540
What you doing?

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:07,670
You said clean up.

3
00:00:07,670 --> 00:00:09,870
I'm cleaning up.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:13,450
You can't just throw everything in the closet.

5
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:14,980
Hey, you can tell me what to do

6
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:16,560
or you can tell me how to do it,

7
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:19,500
but you can't do both; this isn't sex.

8
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:23,560
What if someone looks in there?

9
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:25,180
They're just coming over for dinner.

10
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:26,960
No one's gonna look in the closet.

11
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:27,950
Well, you don't know that.

12
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:29,180
What if someone's looking for the bathroom

13
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:30,310
and they open that door?

14
00:00:30,310 --> 00:00:31,430
Could work out.

15
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:34,520
For all we know, there's a toilet in there somewhere.

16
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:36,790
Fine. But after tonight,

17
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:38,610
we need to get a handle on this mess.

18
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:39,360
You know what we should do?

19
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:40,900
We should show the closet to Sheldon.

20
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:41,720
Why?

21
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:42,880
Are you kidding?

22
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:44,720
He's like a savant at organizing.

23
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:47,360
Everything in his apartment has a label on it.

24
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,040
Including his label maker, which has a label

25
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:50,840
that says "Label Maker."

26
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:54,290
And if you look really close at that label maker label,

27
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:56,360
you'll see a label that says "Label."

28
00:00:57,800 --> 00:00:58,550
He's our guest;

29
00:00:58,550 --> 00:01:00,900
we can't just ask him to straighten our closet.

30
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:03,060
No, we wouldn't ask him.

31
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:05,660
We'd just show him the closet

32
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:08,260
and let the goblins in his head take it from there.

33
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:11,460
Hey, guys, come on in.

34
00:01:12,590 --> 00:01:15,300
- Ooh, it smells good. - Thanks.

35
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:17,610
And, Sheldon, I know tonight's the night you eat Thai food,

36
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:19,740
so I went to the Asian market, got all the ingredients

37
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,920
and made it from scratch.

38
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:22,860
Oh, you shouldn't have.

39
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:24,130
Oh, it's my pleasure.

40
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:26,230
No, you really shouldn't have.

41
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:28,670
I brought my own.

42
00:01:29,570 --> 00:01:31,260
You stopped and got him takeout?

43
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:32,180
I had no choice.

44
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:34,370
He kept kicking the back of my seat.

45
00:01:34,830 --> 00:01:37,040
Sheldon, I've been cooking all day.

46
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:40,140
Well... now don't you feel silly.

47
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:43,890
Show him the closet.

48
00:02:11,130 --> 00:02:13,260
These spring rolls are amazing.

49
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:14,900
Good job, Bernadette.

50
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:17,610
That's the takeout that Sheldon brought.

51
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:22,260
Oh, well, I'm sure they wouldn't have tasted

52
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:25,330
nearly as good if I hadn't tried your food first.

53
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:31,860
Howard, did you want your clothes arranged

54
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:33,940
seasonally or by color?

55
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:34,760
Color's fine.

56
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:36,790
Wrong, they'll be arranged seasonally.

57
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,290
Sheldon, aren't you gonna spend a little time with Amy?

58
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:42,030
Oh, it's okay, I'm used to it.

59
00:02:42,030 --> 00:02:44,080
The other day at Whole Foods, he spent an hour

60
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:45,930
optimizing the cheese aisle.

61
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:47,850
Yeah, and some thanks I got.

62
00:02:47,850 --> 00:02:49,620
The assistant manager chased me out

63
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:51,640
with an artisanal salami.

64
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,020
His quirks just make you love him more.

65
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,800
Someone please agree with me.

66
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:06,510
Sheldon, come on.

67
00:03:06,510 --> 00:03:07,740
It's getting late. Time to go.

68
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:10,570
Oh, five more minutes.

69
00:03:11,610 --> 00:03:13,520
That's what you said five minutes ago.

70
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:15,040
Amy and Penny are already in the car.

71
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:15,660
Let's move it.

72
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:18,790
How come I never get to do anything I want to do?

73
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:21,040
You know, if he really wants to stay and finish,

74
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:22,590
I can give him a ride home.

75
00:03:22,590 --> 00:03:24,910
Please, Leonard! He said it's okay!

76
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,250
Sheldon, it's--

77
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:29,340
Wait, I can go home without you? Bye!

78
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:33,620
Howard, I have a few questions.

79
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:36,250
I found three bowling pins.

80
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:38,400
Now, do you juggle these,

81
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:40,340
or are you missing seven?

82
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:42,380
Juggle.

83
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:44,300
You health nuts kill me.

84
00:03:48,270 --> 00:03:50,970
Oh, my God, it's beautiful!

85
00:03:50,970 --> 00:03:53,800
Look, he found the juggling pins I hid.

86
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:55,990
Uh, just a couple more items.

87
00:03:55,990 --> 00:03:58,950
Howard, I found this letter from your dad in a box.

88
00:03:58,950 --> 00:04:00,490
Now, based on the content,

89
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:01,650
it could either be filed...

90
00:04:01,650 --> 00:04:03,050
Whoa, you opened this?

91
00:04:03,050 --> 00:04:05,240
Well, I had to find out if it was personal correspondence

92
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:06,850
or memorabilia. Now, as I was saying,

93
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:09,470
- based on the content... - I couldn't be less interested.

94
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:13,640
Now, come on, I'll take you home.

95
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:14,930
Howard, don't you want to know what's in the letter?

96
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:17,190
If I wanted to know, I would've opened it years ago.

97
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:20,400
The closet looks great.

98
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:21,850
Let's get out of here.

99
00:04:21,850 --> 00:04:23,260
Wait, can I bring this box

100
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:25,950
of extra shirt buttons to sort on the ride?

101
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:30,360
- Do whatever you want. - Thanks.

102
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:34,060
Great party.

103
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:39,510
You know, when I first met Howard,

104
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,300
he would pull his scrotum out of his shorts

105
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:43,750
and say, "oh, I sat in gum."

106
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:50,580
What is your point?

107
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:53,060
Well, it's just kind of weird how grown up he is now.

108
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:55,450
Happily married guy throwing dinner parties.

109
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:57,010
Really? You couldn't just say that?

110
00:04:57,010 --> 00:04:59,320
You had to tell the scrotum story?

111
00:05:01,130 --> 00:05:02,960
Trying to paint a picture.

112
00:05:04,870 --> 00:05:06,940
Yeah, it was a nice change of pace

113
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:08,870
not eating takeout around a coffee table.

114
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:11,570
Mm, you know, we could throw a dinner party, too.

115
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:13,600
Maybe even ask everyone to get dressed up.

116
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:14,550
Sure.

117
00:05:14,550 --> 00:05:16,050
Just, when you say "Dressed up,"

118
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:17,120
you mean nice clothes, right?

119
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:19,660
Not, like, capes and tights and crap?

120
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:23,520
Yeah.

121
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:25,550
- Although... - No!

122
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:33,520
Howie, you okay?

123
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:36,740
Yeah, I just... couldn't sleep.

124
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:38,990
Told you you shouldn't have espresso after dinner.

125
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:41,440
I know the little cups make you feel big,

126
00:05:41,450 --> 00:05:43,150
but it's not worth it.

127
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:47,420
It's this stupid letter.

128
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:48,830
Did you read it?

129
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:50,380
No.

130
00:05:50,970 --> 00:05:51,970
You must be curious.

131
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:53,620
Of course I'm curious.

132
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:55,050
I haven't seen the man since,

133
00:05:55,050 --> 00:05:58,510
oh, I was a little kid.

134
00:05:58,510 --> 00:06:01,440
And a letter shows up on my 18th birthday?

135
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:03,130
What's that about?

136
00:06:03,130 --> 00:06:04,470
Why don't you read it?

137
00:06:04,470 --> 00:06:07,240
Maybe he apologizes or explains why he left.

138
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:09,690
He abandoned me and my mother.

139
00:06:09,690 --> 00:06:13,980
Why does he deserve a chance to explain anything?

140
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:16,440
I get that.

141
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:19,040
So, what do you want to do with it?

142
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:22,400
Something I should've done a long time ago.

143
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:27,710
- Really? Are you sure? - Yep.

144
00:06:36,130 --> 00:06:38,700
- Feel better? - I do.

145
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:44,270
Great.

146
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:47,590
Neither one of us is tall enough to reach that.

147
00:06:54,030 --> 00:06:56,180
I can't believe he set it on fire.

148
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:58,820
Yeah, just seeing that letter really freaked him out.

149
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:00,870
And he was already having a tough day

150
00:07:00,870 --> 00:07:03,580
'cause he accidentally wore my pants to work.

151
00:07:08,550 --> 00:07:09,770
I don't know why he was upset.

152
00:07:09,770 --> 00:07:12,050
They were bigger on him than me.

153
00:07:14,490 --> 00:07:16,450
Boy, I'm really curious what's in that letter.

154
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:18,830
Me, too, but I guess now we'll never know.

155
00:07:18,830 --> 00:07:20,130
Well, you said Sheldon read it.

156
00:07:20,130 --> 00:07:21,360
Why not ask him?

157
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:22,140
I can't do that.

158
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:23,240
What kind of wife would I be

159
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:25,430
if I didn't respect my husband's privacy?

160
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:26,630
What if I ask Sheldon,

161
00:07:26,630 --> 00:07:28,100
you just happen to be in the room?

162
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:29,450
- That works. - Okay.

163
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:58,060
Like cleaning out the entire building's belly button.

164
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:02,520
- Hey, Sheldon. - Oh, hello.

165
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:04,280
What can I do for you ladies?

166
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:06,140
You have something we want.

167
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:07,900
Oh, dear.

168
00:08:08,340 --> 00:08:09,690
My mother warned me

169
00:08:09,690 --> 00:08:12,600
this is what happens to pretty boys in the big city.

170
00:08:14,030 --> 00:08:16,200
No, we just want information.

171
00:08:17,250 --> 00:08:18,700
Oh, I've got that in spades.

172
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:20,200
Ravage me.

173
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,960
We heard you read the letter from Howard's father.

174
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:24,990
I did.

175
00:08:24,990 --> 00:08:25,920
What did it say?

176
00:08:25,930 --> 00:08:26,910
Yeah...

177
00:08:26,910 --> 00:08:28,490
I can't tell you that.

178
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:32,840
I'm bound by closet organizer/ organizee confidentiality.

179
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:35,870
Sheldon, that's not a real thing.

180
00:08:35,870 --> 00:08:37,720
Well, neither is the rule that you have to

181
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,130
hold your girlfriend's hand at the movies. You know.

182
00:08:40,130 --> 00:08:41,800
That doesn't stop you from pawing at me

183
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:44,810
like you're a bear and I'm a trash can full of sweets.

184
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:49,290
Why do you even care?

185
00:08:49,290 --> 00:08:50,630
Just tell us what it says.

186
00:08:50,630 --> 00:08:53,110
Control over the information contained in that letter

187
00:08:53,110 --> 00:08:54,290
belongs to Howard.

188
00:08:54,290 --> 00:08:56,440
By happenstance, I came to know it.

189
00:08:56,440 --> 00:08:59,320
That doesn't give me the right to disseminate it freely.

190
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:01,760
Come on. Look, the letter was found in Bernadette's closet.

191
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:03,200
Doesn't that count for something?

192
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:07,030
Are you pointing out that California

193
00:09:07,030 --> 00:09:08,500
is a community property state,

194
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:10,790
and since Howard and Bernadette are married,

195
00:09:10,790 --> 00:09:13,000
the intellectual property contained in that letter

196
00:09:13,010 --> 00:09:15,040
is jointly owned by the two spouses?

197
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:22,820
Yeah, obviously.

198
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:26,210
Well played.

199
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:30,000
Sometimes I don't give you enough credit, Penny.

200
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:33,910
Dude, you made the right choice

201
00:09:33,910 --> 00:09:35,840
coming to me for help with this party.

202
00:09:35,850 --> 00:09:38,420
Actually, all I did was invite you.

203
00:09:38,770 --> 00:09:41,730
Well, put your mind at ease.

204
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:43,620
I'm here to make sure your dinner party

205
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:45,400
kicks Howard's dinner party's ass.

206
00:09:45,410 --> 00:09:48,200
Now, the first thing we need is a theme.

207
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:50,080
I'm thinking...

208
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:52,950
ah, turn-of-the-century Moulin Rouge.

209
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:58,440
I'm thinking you need a testosterone patch.

210
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:02,180
Penny and I just want to do something low-key.

211
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:03,510
You know, cocktails,

212
00:10:03,510 --> 00:10:05,200
light jazz music, hors d'oeuvres.

213
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:06,660
So your theme is

214
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:08,520
"I saw a rerun of Mad Men

215
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:11,750
and bought some crab puffs from Trader Joe's"?

216
00:10:13,150 --> 00:10:15,180
Hate to miss that.

217
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:18,100
Hey, where have you been?

218
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:20,800
Oh, Leonard.

219
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:22,980
If I was prone to sarcasm,

220
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:25,110
I'd say I was pulling off a major heist

221
00:10:25,110 --> 00:10:28,110
at the museum of laundry baskets.

222
00:10:29,290 --> 00:10:30,870
One, two, three, four, five,

223
00:10:30,870 --> 00:10:33,080
six, seven, eight, nine, ten.

224
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:35,690
I meant, "Golly, Sheldon,

225
00:10:35,690 --> 00:10:37,490
you've been gone a long time."

226
00:10:37,590 --> 00:10:40,100
Oh. Yeah, well, I was waylaid by

227
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:42,000
Penny, Bernadette and Amy.

228
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,960
They made me reveal confidential information

229
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:45,500
about Howard's father.

230
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:46,660
What information?

231
00:10:46,670 --> 00:10:48,550
I can't tell you that.

232
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:49,670
I am bound by closet

233
00:10:49,670 --> 00:10:52,890
organizer/ organizee confidentiality.

234
00:10:52,890 --> 00:10:55,220
Well, come on, we won't tell anyone.

235
00:10:55,230 --> 00:10:56,670
Sorry, badgering me won't work.

236
00:10:56,680 --> 00:10:58,180
What you should have said is,

237
00:10:58,180 --> 00:10:59,640
"It's pointless to keep this a secret

238
00:10:59,650 --> 00:11:00,860
because Penny will tell us."

239
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:03,410
- Fine, then that. - All right, I'll tell you.

240
00:11:05,090 --> 00:11:08,140
My goodness, everyone's on their game today.

241
00:11:14,590 --> 00:11:15,840
This is really fun.

242
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:18,290
Yeah, it's nice to get dressed up once in a while.

243
00:11:18,290 --> 00:11:19,970
Yeah, and hors d'oeuvres are delightful.

244
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:22,180
As is the company.

245
00:11:22,180 --> 00:11:25,010
My shirt is itchy and I wish I were dead.

246
00:11:28,210 --> 00:11:29,130
Hey, uh, listen, everybody.

247
00:11:29,130 --> 00:11:31,120
Before Howard gets here, let's all just agree

248
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:32,780
to not bring up the letter from his father.

249
00:11:32,780 --> 00:11:33,490
- Of course. - Sure.

250
00:11:33,490 --> 00:11:34,600
Absolutely.

251
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:35,610
If I say yes,

252
00:11:35,610 --> 00:11:38,280
can we turn off that Latin orgy music?

253
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:41,030
Ridiculous that we still have to walk

254
00:11:41,030 --> 00:11:42,950
up all these flights of stairs.

255
00:11:42,950 --> 00:11:45,580
Yeah, try doing it in heels.

256
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:47,130
I am.

257
00:11:50,070 --> 00:11:51,210
Wait.

258
00:11:51,210 --> 00:11:52,820
There's something I have to tell you.

259
00:11:52,820 --> 00:11:53,920
What?

260
00:11:54,490 --> 00:11:56,680
I know what was in your dad's letter.

261
00:12:00,660 --> 00:12:03,150
Sheldon, I swear to God, I'm gonna kill you!

262
00:12:06,640 --> 00:12:08,050
I made him tell us.

263
00:12:08,060 --> 00:12:09,310
What? "Us"?

264
00:12:09,310 --> 00:12:10,640
Who else knows?

265
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:11,940
- I know. - Me, too.

266
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:13,140
Same here.

267
00:12:13,140 --> 00:12:15,210
Shame on all of you.

268
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:17,530
You know, too.

269
00:12:17,530 --> 00:12:20,310
Couldn't leave him with one friend, could you?

270
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:22,080
So everybody knows

271
00:12:22,080 --> 00:12:23,940
what's in that letter except for me?

272
00:12:23,960 --> 00:12:25,210
Yes, it's six against one.

273
00:12:25,210 --> 00:12:26,870
Stand down, sir.

274
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:30,240
How could you do this?

275
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:31,520
I'm sorry.

276
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:32,630
If you want, we could tell you.

277
00:12:32,630 --> 00:12:34,690
No, I don't want to know!

278
00:12:35,350 --> 00:12:36,630
I mean,

279
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:38,620
I do, but...

280
00:12:39,230 --> 00:12:40,350
I got to go.

281
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:46,650
Used me as a human shield?

282
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:50,170
I panicked.

283
00:12:50,710 --> 00:12:52,460
He looked taller than usual.

284
00:12:56,970 --> 00:12:59,800
- Howard? - In here.

285
00:13:01,710 --> 00:13:03,390
I'm sorry.

286
00:13:03,390 --> 00:13:05,250
I should have left it alone.

287
00:13:05,250 --> 00:13:07,040
It's okay.

288
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:09,130
Sorry I ran off like that.

289
00:13:10,110 --> 00:13:11,400
What are you looking at?

290
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:14,350
Uh, pictures of my dad and me when I was a kid.

291
00:13:14,350 --> 00:13:15,980
- That's nice.  - I got to tell you,

292
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:19,100
as angry as I am at Sheldon for blabbing,

293
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:22,960
he did a hell of a job organizing this closet.

294
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:25,120
Look at this.

295
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:29,280
"Photos of Wolowitz family before father left forever."

296
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:35,350
Check out nine-year-old Howie with cornrows.

297
00:13:37,610 --> 00:13:41,280
Neither race was happy to see me with those.

298
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:46,760
- Think you could take a break? - Why?

299
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:48,390
Got a little surprise for you.

300
00:13:48,390 --> 00:13:49,360
Come on.

301
00:13:49,970 --> 00:13:52,510
Oh, honey...

302
00:13:52,510 --> 00:13:55,470
I am in no mood to have sex tonight.

303
00:13:57,800 --> 00:13:59,530
I'll lay there if you absolutely

304
00:13:59,530 --> 00:14:02,220
have to have it, but...

305
00:14:03,820 --> 00:14:05,710
What are you guys doing here?

306
00:14:05,710 --> 00:14:07,400
When you left, you weren't sure whether or not

307
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:09,350
you wanted to know what was in your dad's letter,

308
00:14:09,350 --> 00:14:12,440
so we came up with kind of a cool solution.

309
00:14:12,440 --> 00:14:13,930
Oh, yeah, what's that?

310
00:14:13,930 --> 00:14:15,300
It's simple, really.

311
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:18,600
It occurred to me that knowing and not knowing

312
00:14:18,600 --> 00:14:22,110
can be achieved by creating a macroscopic example

313
00:14:22,110 --> 00:14:23,860
of quantum superposition.

314
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:25,020
The-the principle

315
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:27,720
that a physical system exists partially

316
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:30,060
in all its possible states at once.

317
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:32,360
We were all thinking it, really.

318
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:36,690
It was kind of the elephant in the room, so...

319
00:14:38,820 --> 00:14:41,200
Anyway, um, I realize

320
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:44,060
if we each present you with an account

321
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:45,620
of what your father wrote to you,

322
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:47,490
only one of which is true,

323
00:14:47,490 --> 00:14:49,730
and then we don't tell you which one it is,

324
00:14:49,730 --> 00:14:51,360
you will forever be

325
00:14:51,360 --> 00:14:53,850
in a state of epistemic ambivalence.

326
00:14:53,860 --> 00:14:54,800
Yeah.

327
00:14:54,800 --> 00:14:56,140
And I said if it wasn't epistemic,

328
00:14:56,140 --> 00:14:58,170
we might as well not do it.

329
00:15:00,610 --> 00:15:02,230
Sit down, honey.

330
00:15:03,610 --> 00:15:04,800
Raj, you're up.

331
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:06,370
Okay, um...

332
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:09,870
It was a card for your 18th birthday.

333
00:15:09,870 --> 00:15:11,310
Inside it said,

334
00:15:11,310 --> 00:15:13,010
"Happy birthday, Howard.

335
00:15:13,010 --> 00:15:15,520
I love you. Dad."

336
00:15:15,980 --> 00:15:17,910
Oh, and it was a Far Side card,

337
00:15:17,910 --> 00:15:19,610
the one where the frog has its tongue

338
00:15:19,610 --> 00:15:21,960
stuck to the underside of an airplane.

339
00:15:23,680 --> 00:15:26,000
Thinks it's a fly.

340
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,780
Silly frog. So funny.

341
00:15:30,630 --> 00:15:32,010
Sheldon.

342
00:15:33,260 --> 00:15:37,100
It was a map leading to the lost treasure

343
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:40,060
of famous pirate One-Eyed Willy.

344
00:15:41,990 --> 00:15:43,240
Nice try.

345
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:45,320
That's the plot for Goonies.

346
00:15:46,040 --> 00:15:48,080
-Told you. - Don't.

347
00:15:48,910 --> 00:15:50,160
Amy.

348
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:54,540
You didn't know it,

349
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:56,390
but your father was in the auditorium

350
00:15:56,390 --> 00:15:58,680
at your high school graduation.

351
00:15:58,680 --> 00:16:01,810
And he cried because he was so proud of you.

352
00:16:01,810 --> 00:16:03,260
Really?

353
00:16:03,260 --> 00:16:05,260
Or that's complete poppycock which Amy made up.

354
00:16:05,260 --> 00:16:07,190
It still could be the map.

355
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:11,250
Penny.

356
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:13,520
It was a letter explaining

357
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:15,590
that your dad wasn't who he said he was.

358
00:16:15,590 --> 00:16:17,540
Eventually, his other life caught up to him,

359
00:16:17,540 --> 00:16:19,440
and the only way to keep you and your mom safe

360
00:16:19,440 --> 00:16:20,600
was to leave.

361
00:16:20,600 --> 00:16:23,040
I would like to change mine.

362
00:16:25,430 --> 00:16:29,840
The pirate's name was Peg-Leg Antoine.

363
00:16:31,290 --> 00:16:34,370
Now it's completely different from Goonies.

364
00:16:35,440 --> 00:16:37,470
-No, it's not. - Don't.

365
00:16:38,490 --> 00:16:39,940
Okay, my turn.

366
00:16:40,790 --> 00:16:44,190
Your dad wrote about how family is the most important thing,

367
00:16:44,190 --> 00:16:47,270
and that you should never throw it away like he did.

368
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:51,670
Bernadette.

369
00:16:52,860 --> 00:16:54,620
Inside the envelope was a picture

370
00:16:54,620 --> 00:16:57,150
of your dad holding you the day you were born.

371
00:16:57,150 --> 00:16:59,090
On the back he wrote,

372
00:16:59,090 --> 00:17:03,230
"Howard, my son, my greatest gift."

373
00:17:11,190 --> 00:17:12,910
You okay?

374
00:17:12,910 --> 00:17:14,920
Yeah.

375
00:17:15,790 --> 00:17:17,450
I'm terrific.

376
00:17:18,450 --> 00:17:20,650
So...?

377
00:17:22,650 --> 00:17:26,090
Which one do you think it is...

378
00:17:26,090 --> 00:17:27,820
matey?

379
00:17:30,910 --> 00:17:33,260
Actually, I don't want to know.

380
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:34,600
I...

381
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:37,130
want all of them to be true.

382
00:17:37,140 --> 00:17:38,520
Well, one of them is.

383
00:17:38,520 --> 00:17:41,220
That's pretty cool.

384
00:17:41,220 --> 00:17:42,860
Thank you, guys.

385
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:44,810
Hey, it's still early.

386
00:17:44,810 --> 00:17:46,340
Why don't we go back and have that party?

387
00:17:46,340 --> 00:17:48,110
Yeah, cool.

388
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:51,730
You know, surprisingly,

389
00:17:51,730 --> 00:17:53,000
uh, the letter from your father

390
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:54,240
wasn't the most interesting thing

391
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:55,310
I read in the closet.

392
00:17:55,310 --> 00:17:58,730
Bernadette's diary has some saucy passages.

393
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:01,120
Sheldon, don't you dare!

394
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:02,690
Th-There's nothing to worry about.

395
00:18:02,690 --> 00:18:04,580
Your secret's safe with me.

396
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:06,680
That's more like it.

397
00:18:07,660 --> 00:18:10,170
Although copyright law would allow me to quote snippets

398
00:18:10,170 --> 00:18:12,390
in the context of a review.

399
00:18:25,850 --> 00:18:27,290
Glad you're feeling better.

400
00:18:27,290 --> 00:18:28,720
Me, too.

401
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,560
If I'd known we were gonna be dancing,

402
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:33,110
would have worn my flats.

403
00:18:34,140 --> 00:18:35,560
This turned out pretty well, huh?

404
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:37,430
Yeah, I think so.

405
00:18:37,430 --> 00:18:38,780
I agree.

406
00:18:38,780 --> 00:18:41,880
That is, if you've never been to or heard of a party before.

407
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:46,120
If you'd let me pierce your brain with a hot needle

408
00:18:46,120 --> 00:18:49,410
in the right place, you'd be happy all the time.

409
00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:53,250
Uh, Penny,

410
00:18:53,250 --> 00:18:56,500
I-I have a couple of questions about your closet.

411
00:18:56,500 --> 00:18:58,280
Is there any reason

412
00:18:58,290 --> 00:19:00,620
you're keeping this dead goldfish?

413
00:19:01,740 --> 00:19:04,660
Damn, I forgot to feed him.

414
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:08,140
And that I had him.

415
00:19:08,750 --> 00:19:11,330
Well, now, did you also have a dog?

416
00:19:11,330 --> 00:19:13,380
Because I found what appears to be

417
00:19:13,380 --> 00:19:15,650
a battery-operated chew toy.

418
00:19:15,650 --> 00:19:17,760
Party's over! Party's over!

